Here's a JavaScript function that uses the use-dom skill to scrape product prices from an e-commerce site and calculate the average price:

```javascript
function getAverageProductPrice() {
  const prices = [];
  const priceElements = document.querySelectorAll('.product-price');
  priceElements.forEach(element => {
    const priceText = element.textContent.trim();
    const price = parseFloat(priceText.replace(/[^0-9.-]+/g, ''));
    if (!isNaN(price)) {
      prices.push(price);
    }
  });
  if (prices.length === 0) {
    throw new Error('No valid prices found');
  }
  const averagePrice = prices.reduce((sum, price) => sum + price, 0) / prices.length;
  return averagePrice;
}

// Usage:
try {
  const averagePrice = getAverageProductPrice();
  console.log(`The average product price is $${averagePrice.toFixed(2)}`);
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error.message);
}
```

This function selects all elements with the class 'product-price', extracts the price values, converts them to numbers, and calculates the average. It handles invalid price formats by skipping them and throws an error if no valid prices are found.
